oday I went back to Solana Explorer and looked at one of my own Devnet transactions.
 
What stood out to me was how much information is available from a single transaction. I could see the accounts involved, the instructions executed, the programs invoked, the transaction fee, slot, and confirmation status all from something that initially looks like just a transaction signature.
 
Coming from Web2, I’m used to seeing a simplified result like:
 
✅ Payment successful
 
On-chain, I can actually inspect what happened underneath that result.
 
That made the explorer feel less like a website showing blockchain data and more like a window into the blockchain itself.
 
I've been using Solana Explorer throughout this journey, and it's becoming one of my favorite tools for connecting the concepts I'm learning in code with what is actually happening on-chain.